 ## Overview
-Rendering Pipelines and Passes refers to the architectural organization of volume rendering into discrete, sequential stages or passes, typically leveraging hardware-accelerated rasterization to transform volumetric primitives into a 2D image [S1, S3, S4, unofficial]. It solves the performance bottleneck of direct volume rendering by decomposing the complex task of visibility determination, shading, and compositing into manageable, reusable components [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. Unlike monolithic ray-marching shaders or single-pass splatting implementations, this family explicitly separates geometry traversal, attribute computation, and final screen presentation, enabling complex effects like deferred shading, differentiable optimization, and efficient memory management [S1, S2, unofficial][S10, abstract only, unofficial].
+Rendering Pipelines and Passes is a family of object-order rendering methods that decompose volumetric data into geometric primitives (splats) which are transformed, rasterized, and composited to form an image [S1, S3, S4, unofficial]. It solves the performance bottleneck of volume rendering by leveraging hardware-accelerated rasterization and blending, enabling real-time interactivity for dense point sets or voxel data [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. It differs from ray-casting methods by processing primitives in screen space rather than sampling rays per pixel, and from pure mesh-based rendering by handling implicit or unstructured data via splatting kernels [S10, abstract only, unofficial].
 
